<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical} schema={schema}><Breadcrumbs items={[{label:'Home',href:'/'},{label:'Software',href:'/software/'},{label:'Gumroad Alternatives'}]} /><article><header class="section"><div class="container"><p class="eyebrow">Alternatives guide</p><h1>Best Gumroad Alternatives in 2026: Compare the Cost of the Sale, Not the Sticker Price</h1><p class="lede">Gumroad has no monthly subscription, which sounds cheap. The interesting question starts after you make a sale.</p><p class="meta">Scheduled for September 11, 2026 · Facts verified September 10, 2026</p></div></header><div class="narrow prose article-body">
<AffiliateDisclosure hasRelationship={true} text="SelectVerdict has an affiliate relationship with Systeme.io. If you sign up through our Systeme.io link, we may earn a commission at no extra cost to you. That relationship does not determine our conclusions or rankings." />
<p>Most Gumroad-alternative lists compare features first and fees second. We think that is backwards for many creators. Gumroad's defining trade-off is economic: no monthly bill, but a meaningful charge when revenue arrives.</p>
<p>For direct sales, Gumroad currently charges <strong>10% + $0.50 per transaction</strong>, plus sales tax, and its help center says that does not include credit-card processing of 2.9% + $0.30 or PayPal fees. Discover marketplace sales are charged at 30%, with processing included. Once paid sales in a calendar month reach $20,000, <em>new direct sales for the rest of that month</em> move to 5% + $0.50. The lower rate is not retroactive.</p>
<p>That means the right alternative depends on revenue, average order value, tax responsibility and whether you value Gumroad Discover. We did not hands-on test the products for this article. Prices and fees below come from first-party sources checked September 10, 2026; cost examples are our calculations from those published rates.</p>
<h2>Quick verdict</h2><div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Gumroad alternatives"><table><thead><tr><th>Alternative</th><th>Best for</th><th>Pricing model</th><th>Main trade-off</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th>Payhip</th><td>Creators who want low platform fees without feature gating</td><td>$0 + 5%; $29 + 2%; $99 + 0%</td><td>Stripe/PayPal processing remains separate</td></tr>
<tr><th>Lemon Squeezy</th><td>Software/digital sellers wanting Merchant of Record tax handling</td><td>$0/mo ecommerce; 5% + $0.50 base</td><td>Extra fees can apply by payment/region/subscription</td></tr>
<tr><th>Sellfy</th><td>Established stores preferring fixed subscription cost</td><td>$39/$79/$159 monthly; 0% Sellfy transaction fee</td><td>Annual sales limits by plan; processors separate</td></tr>
<tr><th>Systeme.io</th><td>Creators who also need funnels, email and automation</td><td>Free; paid from $17/mo</td><td>Broader marketing stack than a simple storefront</td></tr>
<tr><th>SamCart</th><td>Higher-value offers where checkout optimization matters</td><td>Subscription model</td><td>Not designed as a cheap Gumroad clone</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h2>The fee math Gumroad comparisons often miss</h2>
<p>Take a $50 product. Before Gumroad's $20,000 monthly threshold, ten direct sales ($500 revenue) produce $55 in Gumroad platform charges: 10% of revenue plus ten $0.50 fixed fees. At $1,000 revenue from 20 sales, that becomes $110; at $5,000 from 100 sales, $550; at $10,000 from 200 sales, $1,100. Those figures exclude payment processing.</p>
<p>Using Payhip's published platform rates on the same revenue, Free costs 5% of revenue, Plus costs $29 + 2%, and Pro costs $99 with no Payhip transaction fee. That gives:</p>
<div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Platform fee examples"><table><thead><tr><th>Monthly revenue at $50 AOV</th><th>Gumroad*</th><th>Payhip Free</th><th>Payhip Plus</th><th>Payhip Pro</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>$500 / 10 sales</td><td>$55</td><td>$25</td><td>$39</td><td>$99</td></tr><tr><td>$1,000 / 20</td><td>$110</td><td>$50</td><td>$49</td><td>$99</td></tr><tr><td>$2,000 / 40</td><td>$220</td><td>$100</td><td>$69</td><td>$99</td></tr><tr><td>$5,000 / 100</td><td>$550</td><td>$250</td><td>$129</td><td>$99</td></tr><tr><td>$10,000 / 200</td><td>$1,100</td><td>$500</td><td>$229</td><td>$99</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p class="source-note">*Gumroad example uses the pre-threshold 10% + $0.50 direct-sale platform charge. It excludes card/PayPal processing and does not model the 5% rate that applies only to new direct sales after the $20,000 monthly threshold is crossed.</p>
<p>Two useful breakpoints fall out of Payhip's own pricing. Plus becomes cheaper than Free at about <strong>$966.67 monthly revenue</strong> because $29 + 2% equals 5% there. Pro becomes cheaper than Plus at about <strong>$3,500</strong>, before processor fees. That is a more actionable comparison than simply calling one plan “cheaper.”</p>
<h2>1. Payhip — best pure creator-commerce alternative for fee control</h2>
<p>Payhip is unusually easy to model because all three plans include all features, unlimited products and unlimited revenue. The choice is primarily how you want to pay the platform: 5% on Free, $29 + 2% on Plus, or $99 with no Payhip transaction fee on Pro.</p>
<p>Stripe and PayPal still charge their own processing fees. Payhip says it automatically collects and remits EU and UK VAT; other taxes can be configured and reported. That is different from a global Merchant of Record model, so tax handling should be part of the decision rather than a footnote.</p>
<p><strong>Choose Payhip if:</strong> you want a straightforward storefront and predictable platform-fee breakpoints. <strong>Skip it if:</strong> global Merchant of Record responsibility is a central requirement.</p>
<h2>2. Lemon Squeezy — best when Merchant of Record matters</h2>
<p>Lemon Squeezy has no monthly ecommerce charge and publishes a base fee of 5% + $0.50 per transaction. It acts as Merchant of Record and handles sales-tax/VAT collection and filing.</p>
<p>The base rate is not the whole story. Its fee documentation lists possible additions including 1.5% for international transactions, 1.5% for PayPal and 0.5% for subscriptions. Payout fees can also vary by region and method.</p>
<p>That makes Lemon Squeezy particularly interesting for software, licenses, subscriptions and internationally sold digital goods where tax administration has real value. It is not accurate to say it is simply “half the price of Gumroad” because transaction mix can change the effective rate and Gumroad itself now operates as Merchant of Record.</p>
<h2>3. Sellfy — best when fixed subscription cost beats percentage fees</h2>
<p>Sellfy charges a subscription rather than a Sellfy transaction fee. Its current help documentation lists Starter at $39/month, Business at $79 and Premium at $159; yearly totals are $348, $708 and $1,428. Stripe or PayPal processing remains separate.</p>
<p>The catch is plan capacity. Starter is positioned for up to $10,000 in annual sales, Business up to $50,000 and Premium up to $200,000. Business adds upselling, cart abandonment and affiliate marketing. So the relevant Sellfy plan depends on both annual sales and the marketing tools you need.</p>
<p>For a seller with stable volume, a fixed platform subscription can be easier to budget than a percentage of every sale. For someone with sporadic or near-zero sales, a monthly subscription can be the worse bargain.</p>
<h2>4. Systeme.io — best when Gumroad is only one piece of your stack</h2>
<p>Sometimes the problem is not Gumroad's storefront at all. It is that you are paying separately for selling, email, funnels, courses and automation. Systeme.io approaches that problem from the other direction: its primary pricing page currently lists Free, Startup at $17/month, Webinar at $47 and Unlimited at $97, with marketing and course tools integrated.</p>
<p>This is not a one-for-one Gumroad replacement comparison. It is a stack-consolidation option. If all you want is a lightweight digital checkout, a broader platform may add complexity you do not need. If Gumroad is sitting beside several other paid marketing tools, consolidation can change the economics substantially.</p>
<p><a class="button" href={affiliateUrl} rel="sponsored nofollow">See Systeme.io's Free plan</a></p>
<h2>5. SamCart — best when the checkout itself has high leverage</h2>
<p>SamCart belongs on this list for a different buyer: someone selling higher-value products who cares about order bumps, upsells, A/B testing, subscription recovery and affiliate management. Those functions can matter more than minimizing the platform fee on a $10 download.</p>
<p>That also means SamCart should not be sold as a universal Gumroad replacement. For inexpensive files and simple memberships, it can be far more platform than you need. For a $500 offer, improving the selling workflow can be economically more important than shaving a few percentage points from a low-ticket transaction.</p>
<h2>Tax handling can outweigh the fee table</h2>
<p>Gumroad says it has operated as Merchant of Record since January 1, 2025 and handles sales-tax collection and remittance worldwide. Lemon Squeezy also operates as Merchant of Record. Payhip automatically handles EU/UK VAT but describes other taxes differently. Sellfy relies on Stripe/PayPal processing and should not be assumed to provide the same global tax-liability model.</p>
<p>Those are structurally different services. A lower headline transaction fee is not automatically cheaper if it transfers compliance work back to the seller.</p>
<h2>Who should stay with Gumroad?</h2>
<p>Gumroad still makes sense when you value zero monthly subscription cost, want its existing workflow, use Discover as an acquisition channel, or value Merchant of Record tax handling enough that migrating is not worth the operational disruption. Its pricing is expensive in some direct-sale scenarios, but cost is not the only switching variable.</p>
<h2>Verdict</h2>
<p><strong>Payhip is our first comparison for a creator primarily trying to reduce platform fees.</strong> Lemon Squeezy is more compelling when Merchant of Record and software/subscription commerce matter. Sellfy makes sense when stable sales justify a fixed subscription. Systeme.io is the more interesting move when the real goal is replacing several creator-marketing tools, not just Gumroad. SamCart is a specialist choice for higher-value selling workflows.</p>
<p>Do the math with your own revenue and average order value before moving. A platform that is cheapest at $500 per month can stop being cheapest at $5,000—and the tax model can change the answer again.</p>
